# BP022 — Evolution Rules

**Status:** Draft

**Version:** 1.0

**Author:** Damien & ChatGPT

**Last Updated:** 2026-07-03

---

# Purpose

This blueprint defines how Waypoint evolves.

Its purpose is to ensure that the platform grows deliberately rather than reactively.

Every abstraction introduces cost.

Every feature increases complexity.

This document describes the principles used to decide when change is justified.

---

# Guiding Principle

> Software should evolve because reality becomes more complex, not because possibility exists.

Waypoint should remain as simple as today's requirements allow.

Future possibilities are acknowledged, but not implemented prematurely.

---

# Rule 1 — Build Today's Behaviour

Design for tomorrow.

Implement for today.

Future requirements should influence interfaces and naming.

They should not introduce speculative code.

Good:

```
resolveCheckIn()

↓

performCheckIn()
```

Poor:

```
ICheckInProvider

BaseCheckInProvider

QrCheckInProvider

GpsCheckInProvider
```

before GPS exists.

---

# Rule 2 — Extract on the First Exception

An abstraction is justified when behaviour diverges.

Not when duplication first appears.

Example:

Today:

```
One visit per venue day.
```

No policy required.

Future:

```
One visit per venue day.

except...

One business allows unlimited visits.
```

Now the rule has exceptions.

The behaviour deserves a home.

Extract:

```
VisitPolicy
```

---

# Rule 3 — One Source of Truth

Every fact has one owner.

Everything else derives from it.

Examples:

Visits own visit history.

Passports derive memories.

Achievements derive progress.

Statistics derive counts.

Never duplicate ownership.

---

# Rule 4 — Opaque Before Trusted

Encapsulation and trust are different concerns.

Today:

```
Opaque token

↓

Client generated
```

Tomorrow:

```
Signed token

↓

Server generated
```

Never create signatures that cannot establish trust.

An unsigned opaque value is preferable to a misleading signature.

---

# Rule 5 — Behaviour Before Infrastructure

Infrastructure exists to support behaviour.

Never build infrastructure simply because it might become useful.

Examples include:

* scanner abstraction
* caching layers
* messaging
* event buses

These should appear only when behaviour genuinely requires them.

---

# Rule 6 — Services Speak the Domain

Repositories speak SQL.

Services speak Waypoint.

A service should never expose database terminology.

Good:

```
already_visited
```

Poor:

```
duplicate_check_in
```

The domain language belongs to Waypoint rather than the database.

---

# Rule 7 — UI Speaks to People

The interface should describe experiences.

Not implementation.

Good:

```
Welcome back.

You visited here today.
```

Poor:

```
Duplicate QR detected.
```

The walker should never need to understand the implementation.

---

# Rule 8 — Comments Explain Why

Code explains what.

Comments explain why.

Comments should preserve reasoning rather than narrate syntax.

Good:

```
The resolution check is a courtesy.

The write check is the guard.
```

Poor:

```
Check if already visited.
```

---

# Rule 9 — Prefer Composition

Small responsibilities compose.

Large responsibilities fracture.

When a module begins solving multiple unrelated problems, divide responsibilities rather than introducing inheritance.

---

# Rule 10 — Make the Next Change Easier

The purpose of good architecture is not to predict the future.

It is to reduce the cost of adapting to it.

Every change should leave the codebase slightly easier to evolve than before.

---

# Engineering Philosophy

Waypoint deliberately grows through conversation.

Ideas are explored.

Assumptions are challenged.

Alternatives are considered.

Only then is code written.

The objective is not simply correctness.

It is clarity.

---

# Consequences

Following these rules produces:

* fewer abstractions
* stronger domain models
* clearer code reviews
* lower maintenance cost
* better documentation
* more deliberate evolution

Most importantly, it allows Waypoint to remain coherent as it grows.

---

# Summary

Every abstraction should earn its existence.

Every feature should strengthen the product's purpose.

Every line of code should make the next line easier to write.

Software ages well when restraint becomes part of the architecture.
